    "prompt": "An isosceles triangle has two sides of length 2. What is the largest possible area of such a triangle?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\sqrt3$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$2\\sqrt2$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$2\\sqrt3$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Area with the sine",
        "body": "If the angle between the two sides of length 2 is $\\theta$, the area is $\\frac12\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot\\sin\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta \\le 2$, with equality for $\\theta = 90^\\circ$ (a right isosceles triangle with legs 2).\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Base and height",
        "body": "Take one side of length 2 as the base. The other side of length 2 starts at an end of the base, so the height is at most 2 (when it is perpendicular). So the area is at most $\\frac12\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 = 2$, reached by the right isosceles triangle.\n\n**Answer: (B) 2**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry",
      "trigonometry"
    ],
