        "key": "E",
        "text": "2"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Examples and a case check",
        "body": "Possible counts:\n\n- 6: four lines in general position;\n- 5: exactly two of the lines parallel;\n- 4: two pairs of parallel lines;\n- 3: three parallel lines and one line crossing them.\n\nWhy 2 is impossible — sort the lines into classes of parallel lines:\n\n- all 4 parallel: 0 points; classes $3 + 1$: 3 points; classes $2 + 2$: 4 points;\n- classes $2 + 1 + 1$: each of the two single lines crosses both parallel lines; they meet each other only once, so on at least one of the parallel lines they cross at different points, giving at least 3 points;\n- no two parallel: if all four pass through one point there is 1 point; otherwise take two lines meeting at $P$ and a line not through $P$ — it meets them in two further points, so there are at least 3.\n\nSo exactly 2 intersection points never occur.\n\n**Answer: (E) 2**"
      }
    ],
