        "text": "$2^{104}+1$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Last digits",
        "body": "The last digits of $2^n$ repeat with period 4: $2, 4, 8, 6$ for $n \\equiv 1, 2, 3, 0 \\pmod 4$. $2^n + 1$ is divisible by 5 when it ends in 5 (or 0), i.e. when $2^n$ ends in 4: $n \\equiv 2 \\pmod 4$. Of $100, \\dots, 104$, only $102$ works: $2^{102}$ ends in 4, so $2^{102} + 1$ ends in 5.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Modular arithmetic",
        "body": "$2^2 = 4 \\equiv -1 \\pmod 5$, so $2^{102} = (2^2)^{51} \\equiv (-1)^{51} = -1$, giving $2^{102} + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod 5$.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
