    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "There are three boxes, each with 1 white, 1 black, 1 red and 1 blue ball. Charles takes out 1 ball from each box, blindfolded. What is the probability that exactly two of the balls taken out are red?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\frac18$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac19$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\frac{3}{16}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\frac{3}{64}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac{9}{64}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Binomial count",
        "body": "From each box the ball is red with probability $\\frac14$. Choose which 2 of the 3 boxes give red ($3$ ways); then the probability is $\\frac14\\cdot\\frac14\\cdot\\frac34 = \\frac{3}{64}$ for each choice. Total: $3\\cdot\\frac{3}{64} = \\frac{9}{64}$.\n\n**Answer: (E)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Count outcomes",
        "body": "There are $4^3 = 64$ equally likely outcomes. Exactly two red: pick the non-red box (3 ways) and its colour (3 ways): $9$ outcomes. Probability $\\frac{9}{64}$.\n\n**Answer: (E)**"
      }
    ],
